WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:09.402
An equitable idea can become inequitable through procurement, engagement, construction, operations, maintenance, or evaluation.

2
00:00:09.402 --> 00:00:17.214
Equity has to travel through the entire project, from the first definition of need to the long-term distribution of results.

3
00:00:17.214 --> 00:00:24.963
This episode adapts Kristine Miller's Discipline-Specific Professional Design Processes and Equity chapter under CC BY 4.0.

4
00:00:24.963 --> 00:00:32.839
It condenses and paraphrases Miller's work and excludes third-party readings, examples, images, quotations, and linked media.

5
00:00:32.839 --> 00:00:36.631
At initiation, ask who requested the project and who was absent.

6
00:00:36.631 --> 00:00:39.949
At scope, define equity outcomes and decision authority.

7
00:00:39.949 --> 00:00:47.059
At procurement, examine team capacity, community relationships, accessibility, and the resources assigned to engagement.

8
00:00:47.059 --> 00:00:49.548
At design, compare distributional effects.

9
00:00:49.548 --> 00:00:52.392
At implementation, manage disruption and access.

10
00:00:52.392 --> 00:00:54.465
During operation, fund maintenance.

11
00:00:54.465 --> 00:00:58.258
During evaluation, ask affected people whether the result works.

12
00:00:58.258 --> 00:01:01.102
Trust cannot be scheduled as one public meeting.

13
00:01:01.102 --> 00:01:06.671
People need time, accessible choices, honest limits, and evidence that participation mattered.

14
00:01:06.671 --> 00:01:16.329
Community-rooted organizations may hold relationships a project team lacks, but partnership should not mean extracting trust without resources or shared authority.

15
00:01:16.329 --> 00:01:18.366
Create an equity control plan.

16
00:01:18.366 --> 00:01:28.482
For each project phase, name the decision, responsible person, affected groups, engagement method, evidence, success measure, and correction trigger.

17
00:01:28.482 --> 00:01:34.932
Add budget lines for language access, disability access, participation support, and evaluation.

18
00:01:34.932 --> 00:01:37.241
Use triggers rather than promises.

19
00:01:37.241 --> 00:01:48.919
If access falls below a target, if displacement indicators rise, if maintenance is unfunded, or if a burden concentrates in one place, specify who must respond and by when.

20
00:01:48.919 --> 00:01:50.638
Torrance application segment.

21
00:01:50.638 --> 00:01:54.611
This is original independent study, separate from Miller's chapter.

22
00:01:54.611 --> 00:02:00.066
It cites the City of Torrance General Plan page, checked October seventh, twenty twenty-six.

23
00:02:00.066 --> 00:02:10.382
For a hypothetical implementation program in the Land Use or Safety Element, write an equity control plan from policy adoption through capital budgeting and annual reporting.

24
00:02:10.382 --> 00:02:14.888
Identify which actions are City-controlled and which require another agency.

25
00:02:14.888 --> 00:02:20.225
Link every outcome to a responsible department, funding path, schedule, and public report.

26
00:02:20.225 --> 00:02:25.383
Include a correction process if benefits do not reach intended users or burdens emerge.

27
00:02:25.383 --> 00:02:31.727
This exercise does not describe an adopted Torrance program, determine compliance, or provide legal advice.

28
00:02:31.727 --> 00:02:36.698
The full-course takeaway is that equity is a continuous design responsibility.

29
00:02:36.698 --> 00:02:44.856
Ask who benefits, who is burdened, who decides, whose knowledge counts, what history matters, and how results will be corrected.

30
00:02:44.856 --> 00:02:49.062
This adaptation credits Kristine Miller and is licensed CC BY 4.0.

31
00:02:49.062 --> 00:02:57.156
Changes include condensation, paraphrase, reorganization, and eight separately sourced Torrance applications across the series.

32
00:02:57.156 --> 00:03:01.107
Third-party items are excluded, and no endorsement is implied.
